  "claim": "The report of Hess's illegal mid 1941 flight to Scotland is why the editor of Mein Kampf stopped serving Hitler in 1941.",
  "evidencia": "Page: Karlheinz Pintsch. Sentence: It fell to him to report Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Hitler and his recollections and notes have been the subject of debate by historians.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: Appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler in 1933, he served in this position until 1941, when he flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: He was taken prisoner and eventually was convicted of crimes against peace, serving a life sentence until his suicide.\nPage: Mein Kampf. Sentence: The book was edited by Hitler's deputy Rudolf Hess.",
  "evidence_text": "Page: Karlheinz Pintsch. Sentence: It fell to him to report Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Hitler and his recollections and notes have been the subject of debate by historians.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: Appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler in 1933, he served in this position until 1941, when he flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: He was taken prisoner and eventually was convicted of crimes against peace, serving a life sentence until his suicide.\nPage: Mein Kampf. Sentence: The book was edited by Hitler's deputy Rudolf Hess.",
