        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a07df655-dfd8-5211-833a-dc518de9f03a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Karlheinz Pintsch, Rudolf Hess, Adolf Hitler\n- Places: Scotland, United Kingdom\n- Roles: Deputy Führer, Hitler's deputy\n- Products: Mein Kampf\n- Events: Hess's May 1941 flight to Scotland, World War II, Hess's conviction and imprisonment\n- Topics: historical debate\n\nFacts:\n- In 1933, Rudolf Hess was appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler.\n- Rudolf Hess edited Adolf Hitler's book Mein Kampf.\n- In May 1941, Rudolf Hess flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch had to report Rudolf Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Adolf Hitler.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch's recollections and notes about Rudolf Hess's May 1941 flight have been debated by historians.\n- After being taken prisoner, Rudolf Hess was convicted of crimes against peace and served a life sentence until his suicide.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "606b7e20-b0fb-5841-9392-7853501feed2",
        "text": "Page: Karlheinz Pintsch. Sentence: It fell to him to report Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Hitler and his recollections and notes have been the subject of debate by historians.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: Appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler in 1933, he served in this position until 1941, when he flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: He was taken prisoner and eventually was convicted of crimes against peace, serving a life sentence until his suicide.\nPage: Mein Kampf. Sentence: The book was edited by Hitler's deputy Rudolf Hess.",
